From c47d6fdd8e1ef0003402b63e2a108b51bfaac000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dolbaras <dolbaras@localhost>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:03:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] hdmitx: VESA mode tables as in the DMT standard

Checked against VESA DMT (port/tools/check_vesa_tables.py: timing,
encoder registers, clock and PLL of every PC monitor mode):

- 1280x800 had the reduced blanking totals (1440x823) with the 83.5 MHz
  clock of the normal timing: 70 Hz at the sink. 1680x1050 used reduced
  blanking (119 MHz). EDID standard timings and Established Timings III
  name the normal DMT timings, which every monitor listing them takes;
  both modes now use them (1680x831 at 83.5 MHz, 2240x1089 at
  146.25 MHz), encoder tables and vout info to match.
- 1360x768: pixel clock 855000 instead of 85500, so the mode was always
  beyond any range limit and matched no detailed timing.
- 2560x1440: CVT-RB vertical porches are 3/5/33, not 2/5/34.
- sync polarities as in DMT (1024x768 -/-, the normal-blanking modes
  -/+, ...); the encoder took +/+ for every VESA mode but 640x480, now
  it takes the mode's polarity.
- the 3180000 PLL setting (1280x768) gave 3168 MHz: frac 0x200 missing.
- Established Timings III: 1280x960@60 is bit 3 of byte 7 (bit 2 is
  @85), 1440x900@60 (byte 8 bit 5) was not collected.
- line frequencies of a few modes.
